{
 "title": "Reinforcement Learning: An Introduction (2nd edition draft)",
 "byline": "Richard S. Sutton and Andrew G. Barto \u00b7 MIT Press; this PDF is an early \u201cin progress\u201d draft of the second edition",
 "note": "PDF page = printed page + 14. This draft predates the published 2018 edition: several chapters (off-policy approximation, psychology, neuroscience) are one-page placeholders and are left out here, and the later chapters differ from the final book. Summaries are in our own words; the text stays in the PDF.",
 "summary": "The standard introduction to reinforcement learning: an agent learning by trial and error to maximize reward. Part I covers tabular methods: bandits and exploration, finite MDPs and the Bellman equations, dynamic programming, Monte Carlo methods, temporal-difference learning (Sarsa, Q-learning), eligibility traces and TD(\u03bb), and planning with learned models (Dyna, prioritized sweeping, MCTS). Part II extends value prediction and control to function approximation and introduces actor\u2013critic and average-reward methods. A case-study chapter covers TD-Gammon, Samuel\u2019s checkers player, the acrobot, elevator dispatching, channel allocation and job-shop scheduling.",
